---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageFrame from '../components/PageFrame.astro';
import Masthead from '../components/Masthead.astro';
import WorkSection from '../components/WorkSection.astro';
import ActionRow from '../components/ActionRow.astro';
import TextLink from '../components/TextLink.astro';
import Dot from '../components/Dot.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import FactsList from '../components/FactsList.astro';
import CaseLead from '../components/CaseLead.astro';
import MetaLine from '../components/MetaLine.astro';
import PointsList from '../components/PointsList.astro';
const head = `<link rel="icon" href="data:," />`;
const afterFooter = ``;
---
<Base title={"Molter - Gorka Molero Case Study"} description={"Case study for Molter, an agent-to-agent dating experiment: sandboxed agents negotiate compatibility through a five-level progressive disclosure protocol on a Hono/Cloudflare API with Neon, Drizzle, Zod, and tweetnacl encryption."} page="molter">
  <Fragment slot="head" set:html={head} />
  <PageFrame class="sheet">
    <Masthead />
    

      <CaseLead class="lead">
        <MetaLine class="meta eyebrow">Case · agent protocol</MetaLine>
        <h2 class="case-title">Molter</h2>
        <p class="intro">On apps like Tinder, people present a tailored version of themselves, and that almost guarantees bad matches. In Molter, your agent gets to know the real you through a series of questions, then negotiates with another person's agent, fully anonymously. The conversation is ephemeral, and the agent has to discover the secrets of your personality to make a real match. It works with the personal agent you already use, like OpenClaw, Hermes or a Grok bot, and because it's agents talking to agents, they can go on far more dates than a person ever could.</p>
        <MetaLine class="meta chips">Experiment <Dot class="dot">&middot;</Dot> Public repo <Dot class="dot">&middot;</Dot> MVP stage</MetaLine>
      </CaseLead>

      <main>
      <WorkSection class="work">
        <h2>Problem</h2>
        <p>Dating apps fail because humans perform for each other: curated photos, rehearsed bios, calculated responses. You are not meeting a person — you are meeting their marketing. An AI agent that actually knows its human could represent them honestly, if the privacy problem is solved first.</p>
      </WorkSection>

      <WorkSection class="work">
        <h2>What I Built</h2>
        <p>Molter is a protocol and network where sandboxed agents negotiate compatibility on behalf of their humans. Each agent holds full knowledge from a structured interview but can only emit structured signals through a five-level progressive disclosure protocol — even a breach exposes "values honesty, anxious attachment," never raw confessions. The monorepo spans a Hono REST API on Cloudflare Workers, a Next.js landing page, a Remotion demo pipeline, and shared packages for Zod schemas, Drizzle/Neon persistence, tweetnacl encryption, an interview skill, and the sandboxed agent runtime.</p>
        <FactsList class="facts">
          <li><strong>Privacy architecture</strong><span>Full knowledge inside, structured output only: sandboxed sub-agents with a single allowed tool.</span></li>
          <li><strong>Negotiation protocol</strong><span>Five-level progressive disclosure with consent gates instead of algorithmic pre-filtering.</span></li>
          <li><strong>Production tech from day one</strong><span>Edge API, serverless Postgres, typed schemas end to end, E2E-encrypted agent comms, expiring tokens.</span></li>
        </FactsList>
        <Figure class="plate">
          <div class="shot"><img src="./assets/molter-landing.png" alt="Molter landing page: large headline reading 'You're not meeting a person — you're meeting their marketing', subline about curated photos and rehearsed bios, and a 'Be part of the experiment' call to action." loading="lazy" /></div>
          <figcaption>The live-built landing page rendered from the repo (Next.js dev server, local capture). Waitlist flow and copy are real; no user data is involved.</figcaption>
        </Figure>
      </WorkSection>


      <WorkSection class="work">
        <h2>Technical Decisions</h2>
        <PointsList class="points">
          <li>No algorithmic pre-filter: every agent can negotiate with every agent, and cheap protocol levels do the filtering.</li>
          <li>Sandbox constraints as the privacy boundary — the agent runtime allows one tool (API fetch) and denies everything else.</li>
          <li>Structured signals as the only output format, validated with Zod at every boundary.</li>
          <li>Deterministic, resumable negotiation workflows with explicit consent gates at deeper disclosure levels.</li>
          <li>Ephemeral conversations, expiring tokens, and tweetnacl end-to-end encryption as defaults, not add-ons.</li>
        </PointsList>
      </WorkSection>

      <WorkSection class="work">
        <h2>What This Proves</h2>
        <p>Molter is evidence of protocol design and agent-systems thinking: privacy-preserving multi-agent negotiation, monorepo product engineering across API, web, and video pipelines, and the judgment to build a speculative consumer idea on production infrastructure from the first commit.</p>
      </WorkSection>

      <WorkSection class="work">
        <h2>Public Evidence</h2>
        <PointsList class="points">
          <li>Full 154-commit history public at <code>gorkamolero/molter</code>.</li>
          <li>Web, API, and agent-runtime packages build clean on Node 20 — available on request.</li>
          <li>Landing page screenshot captured from a local build.</li>
          <li>Fake-data protocol probe: health and both agent registrations pass in the no-database path.</li>
        </PointsList>
      </WorkSection>

      <WorkSection class="work">
        <h2>Known Boundaries</h2>
        <PointsList class="points">
          <li>MVP stage: the interview-skill package has typecheck errors and the Remotion demo build is currently broken.</li>
          <li>The search-to-match happy path needs a real database; the in-process probe stops at search.</li>
          <li>Honest failures are recorded in the build proof rather than edited out.</li>
        </PointsList>
        <ActionRow class="actions"><TextLink class="button primary" href="./workbench.html">Back to workbench</TextLink></ActionRow>
      </WorkSection>
      </main>
  </PageFrame>
  <Fragment slot="after-footer" set:html={afterFooter} />
</Base>
